  Spec written to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-30-user-identity-design.md[39m (not committed). Please review it and let me
  know if you want any changes before I write the implementation plan.

  Three things in it are worth your attention specifically, because they're where I made a call you could reasonably
  make differently:

  1. [1mNew modules use the [38;5;153m.mjs[39m extension.[0m [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m and [38;5;153msrc/[39m uses CommonJS [38;5;153mrequire[39m, so plain
     [38;5;153m.js[39m files would load as CommonJS under [38;5;153mnode --test[39m and the imports would fail. [38;5;153m.mjs[39m gets ES modules in Node without
     converting your unrelated [38;5;153msrc/[39m files. The browser doesn't care — it takes module semantics from the [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m
     attribute.
  2. [1m[38;5;153mlogin[39m's logic moves out of [38;5;153mapp.js[39m into [38;5;153mauth.mjs[39m.[0m That's wider than the original request, but you chose unit tests,
     and [38;5;153mlogin[39m can't be tested while it sits in a file that touches [38;5;153mdocument[39m at load time. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m keeps only DOM wiring.
  3. [1mA failed login leaves an existing stored identity in place.[0m I picked this so one person's failed attempt can't
     evict another's session; the opposite choice — treat any failed auth as a signal the stored identity is stale — is
     defensible, and it's a one-line difference.

